WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.230
A student's first neural network printed

2
00:00:03.230 --> 00:00:05.790
a loss of nan after three steps.

3
00:00:06.470 --> 00:00:08.730
She had copied the code exactly.

4
00:00:09.600 --> 00:00:11.720
The only change was a learning rate

5
00:00:11.720 --> 00:00:13.640
of ten instead of a tenth.

6
00:00:14.830 --> 00:00:18.590
Today: learning as minimising a loss, how

7
00:00:18.590 --> 00:00:20.970
gradient descent does it, and why the

8
00:00:20.970 --> 00:00:22.610
learning rate trips students up.

9
00:00:23.820 --> 00:00:25.420
Supervised learning picks the

10
00:00:25.420 --> 00:00:27.300
parameters that minimise the

11
00:00:27.300 --> 00:00:29.060
average loss on the training data.

12
00:00:29.660 --> 00:00:32.140
That average is the empirical risk.

13
00:00:32.760 --> 00:00:34.480
We hope it tracks the true

14
00:00:34.480 --> 00:00:35.600
risk on new data.

15
00:00:36.840 --> 00:00:39.800
Gradient descent starts somewhere and

16
00:00:39.800 --> 00:00:41.780
repeatedly steps against the gradient.

17
00:00:42.520 --> 00:00:44.580
Theta becomes theta minus

18
00:00:44.580 --> 00:00:46.180
eta times the gradient.

19
00:00:46.920 --> 00:00:48.340
Eta, the learning rate,

20
00:00:48.580 --> 00:00:49.680
sets the step size.

21
00:00:51.010 --> 00:00:52.970
Here is the loss after fifty

22
00:00:52.970 --> 00:00:54.330
steps on the same problem.

23
00:00:54.980 --> 00:00:57.580
A tiny rate barely moves.

24
00:00:58.310 --> 00:01:00.650
A good rate reaches nearly zero.

25
00:01:01.320 --> 00:01:02.640
A rate that is too big

26
00:01:02.640 --> 00:01:04.720
bounces higher and higher.

27
00:01:06.030 --> 00:01:08.790
Real datasets are large, so we estimate

28
00:01:08.790 --> 00:01:11.150
the gradient from a small random batch.

29
00:01:11.870 --> 00:01:14.230
Stochastic gradient descent is noisy,

30
00:01:14.810 --> 00:01:17.090
but each step is cheap, and the noise

31
00:01:17.090 --> 00:01:19.110
can even help escape flat regions.

32
00:01:20.480 --> 00:01:22.000
Students often think the

33
00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:23.500
gradient points to the minimum.

34
00:01:24.170 --> 00:01:26.390
It only points uphill, locally.

35
00:01:27.190 --> 00:01:29.930
Plot a one-parameter loss, let

36
00:01:29.930 --> 00:01:32.070
students take steps by hand, and they

37
00:01:32.070 --> 00:01:33.910
discover overshooting themselves.

38
00:01:35.210 --> 00:01:35.690
Quick check!

39
00:01:36.330 --> 00:01:38.190
The loss rises every step

40
00:01:38.190 --> 00:01:40.150
and then becomes nan.

41
00:01:40.920 --> 00:01:42.700
What is the most likely cause?

42
00:01:44.090 --> 00:01:45.770
The learning rate is too big.

43
00:01:46.470 --> 00:01:49.550
Each step overshoots the valley and lands

44
00:01:49.550 --> 00:01:51.950
higher up, until the numbers overflow.

45
00:01:52.800 --> 00:01:55.080
Divide it by ten and try again.

46
00:01:56.330 --> 00:01:58.330
In the app, open the gradient lab.

47
00:01:58.950 --> 00:02:01.910
Switch the step size between Tiny, Good

48
00:02:01.910 --> 00:02:05.010
and Too big, and press Run each time.

49
00:02:05.590 --> 00:02:09.910
Watch the steps crawl, settle or explode.

50
00:02:11.370 --> 00:02:11.870
Your turn.

51
00:02:12.480 --> 00:02:15.600
The loss is theta squared, theta is three,

52
00:02:16.020 --> 00:02:17.780
and the learning rate is a tenth.

53
00:02:18.420 --> 00:02:20.320
Where is theta after one step?

54
00:02:21.580 --> 00:02:23.120
At two point four, because

55
00:02:23.120 --> 00:02:24.340
the gradient is six.

56
00:02:25.110 --> 00:02:28.110
Remember: learning minimises empirical

57
00:02:28.110 --> 00:02:32.010
risk, gradient descent steps downhill, and

58
00:02:32.010 --> 00:02:34.170
the learning rate decides whether it

59
00:02:34.170 --> 00:02:34.390
works.

60
00:02:35.690 --> 00:02:37.810
Try it in your next lecture: give

61
00:02:37.810 --> 00:02:39.990
each pair the loss theta squared

62
00:02:39.990 --> 00:02:41.510
and three learning rates.

63
00:02:42.140 --> 00:02:44.500
They take five steps by hand and plot

64
00:02:44.500 --> 00:02:46.780
them, before you show any code.
